    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "When factorising fails"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Not every quadratic splits neatly into factors. The quadratic formula always works and the discriminant tells us in advance what kind of roots to expect."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "2x^2 - 7x + 3",
          "D < 0",
          "D = -4",
          "D = 0",
          "D = b^2 - 4ac",
          "D > 0",
          "quadratic formula",
          "x = (-b ± √D)/2a"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Complete the square on a x squared + b x + c = 0 and we get the quadratic formula.\nThe roots are x = (-b plus or minus the square root of b squared - 4ac) divided by 2a.\nThe expression b squared - 4ac is called the discriminant. We write it as D.\nIf D is greater than 0, the equation has two distinct real roots.\nIf D equals 0, the two roots are equal and real, each equal to -b by 2a.\nIf D is less than 0, there are no real roots, because the square root of a negative number is not real.\nFor 2x squared - 7x + 3 = 0, D = 49 - 24 = 25. So x = (7 plus or minus 5)/4, giving 3 and 1/2.\nFor x squared + 4x + 5, D = 16 - 20 = -4, which is negative, so there are no real roots."
        }
      },
